1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
On every build, you hit the same question.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
How do I write a requirement precise enough

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
that a developer can code it and QA can verify it?

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This lesson covers writing acceptance criteria for Claude,

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
decomposing a feature into turns and checks,

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
and treating edge cases as first class.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's break it down.

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Let's set up three building blocks

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
you'll use for every requirement you write.

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
The first is acceptance criteria.

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Those are the specific checkable conditions

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
that mark a requirement as done,

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
and the trick is writing them so a developer

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
and a QA tester read them exactly the same way.

15
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The second is the input-output contract.

16
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
You state what goes in and what should come back,

17
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
a schema-valid JSON object, for example,

18
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
rather than describing how the prompt gets there.

19
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The third is a measurable signal,

20
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
an observable check that actually grades

21
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
whether the output passed.

22
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Criteria, contract, signal.

23
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Together, they turn a vague wish

24
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
into something you can build against

25
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
and prove you delivered.

26
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Here's a mistake I see constantly.

27
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
People write a functional requirement

28
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
that names a specific prompt. Don't do that.

29
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
A prompt is an implementation detail,

30
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
and implementation details don't belong in requirements.

31
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
What you actually want to specify is the contract.

32
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
State what goes in and what shape comes back.

33
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Say the response is a JSON object with these exact fields,

34
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
and then let structured outputs enforce that schema for you

35
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
instead of leaning on clever wording

36
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
to coax the right format.

37
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
The payoff is freedom.

38
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
As long as the contract holds,

39
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
you can rewrite the prompt, swap an approach,

40
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
or tune anything underneath,

41
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
and the requirement is still satisfied.

42
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Specify the what and leave the how to the implementation.

43
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Let's make the difference between weak

44
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and strong criteria concrete

45
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
because this is where most specs leak.

46
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A vague criterion sounds like

47
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
the answer should look good and be helpful.

48
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
It feels reasonable, but nobody can code to it,

49
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
and two reviewers will argue forever

50
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
about whether a given answer passed.

51
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
A testable criterion sounds like

52
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
the response is valid JSON matching the schema,

53
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
and it cites the source document.

54
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Notice what changed.

55
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
A script can now grade that pass or fail

56
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
the same way every single time. That's the bar.

57
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Push it until a machine could judge it.

58
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
A quick test helps here.

59
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
If you can picture writing an assert statement

60
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
for the criterion, it's testable.

61
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
If you can't, keep rewriting until you can.

62
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
So how do you actually build one of these requirements?

63
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Decompose the feature into three columns,

64
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
turns, outputs, and checks.

65
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Start with the user turns.

66
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Write down what the person sends

67
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
at each step of the interaction.

68
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Then, for each turn, state the expected clod output.

69
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Finally, attach a success check to every output,

70
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
a concrete rule that grades whether it's right.

71
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
When you lay a feature out this way,

72
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
you get a requirement that's testable

73
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
end-to-end, turn-by-turn.

74
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
It also surfaces gaps immediately.

75
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
If you can't name the expected output for a turn,

76
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
or you can't write its check,

77
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
you've found a hole in the spec

78
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
before you wrote a line of code.

79
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Now, the part that separates a demo spec

80
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
from a production one, the unhappy paths.

81
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
There are four kinds worth planning for every time.

82
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
First, edge cases, empty fields,

83
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
enormous inputs, strange formats.

84
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Decide the required behavior

85
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
rather than leaving it to chance.

86
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Second, refusal handling.

87
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Clod may decline an unsafe request,

88
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
so plan what your application does

89
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
when the stop reason comes back as a refusal.

90
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Third, out-of-scope inputs.

91
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Requests outside this feature's job.

92
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Define a graceful decline so your scope stays bounded.

93
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
And fourth, ambiguous requests.

94
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
When details are vague or missing,

95
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
specify whether clod asks for clarification

96
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
or falls back on a documented default.

97
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Write these four into the requirement,

98
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
not as afterthoughts.

99
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Let's give refusal handling the attention it deserves

100
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
because it's a requirement, not an edge note.

101
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
When safety classifiers step in,

102
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,000
the response comes back with a refusal stop reason.

103
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
But clod can also decline conversationally

104
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
in the reply text with a normal end turn,

105
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
so plan for both.

106
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Branching only on refusal

107
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
misses those conversational declines.

108
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Your application has to know what to do with either case,

109
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
so write it into the spec.

110
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Decide the exact user-facing message.

111
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Decide whether there's a fallback path.

112
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
And log every refusal,

113
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
so you can measure how often it happens in production.

114
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Here's the mindset shift.

115
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
A refusal you planned for and handled cleanly is a feature.

116
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
A refusal that slips through

117
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
and shows the user a raw error is a bug.

118
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Same event, and the requirement decides which one you ship.

119
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Every requirement you write

120
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
should map to a measurable signal.

121
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Let's look at three common ones.

122
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
The first is a format signal.

123
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Does the output parse and match the schema?

124
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
A JSON validator answers that automatically,

125
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
no human judgment required.

126
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
The second is a grounding signal.

127
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Does the answer cite the source it was handed?

128
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,000
You check that the quoted spans

129
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
actually appear in the document you supplied.

130
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
The third is a behavior signal.

131
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Did the app take the right action, route correctly,

132
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
or refuse when it should have?

133
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
You compare its choices against a labeled set of examples.

134
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Format, grounding, behavior.

135
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Those three cover most requirements you'll write.

136
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Let's close the loop on why every requirement

137
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
needs a measurable signal.

138
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
It's about keeping scope under control.

139
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,000
If you can't grade a requirement,

140
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
you genuinely can't tell when it's done,

141
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
which means it can expand forever and the project slips.

142
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
When every requirement carries a concrete check,

143
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
scope creep becomes visible almost immediately.

144
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Because a new ask either has a signal or it doesn't,

145
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
there's a bonus too.

146
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Each of those success checks

147
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
becomes a real test in your suite later.

148
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
So the work you do defining criteria

149
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
pays you back during implementation.

150
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Bounded and gradable isn't bureaucracy.

151
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
It's how a spec stays honest

152
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
and how you protect yourself from a scope

153
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:37,000
that quietly balloons.

154
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Here's the bottom line.

155
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
A strong functional requirement

156
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
is a contract with a testable check, edge cases included.

157
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Specify the input output contract instead of the prompt.

158
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Decompose features into turns, outputs, and checks,

159
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
and give every requirement a measurable signal.

160
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Just remember, if you can't grade it, it isn't done.

161
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
That wraps up writing functional requirements for Claude.

162
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
When you're ready, I'll see you in the next lesson.

